  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Roy James Carver turned a German tyre-retreading process into the basis of Bandag, but buying the rights was only the beginning. Making the system commercially reliable required further invention. He devised a flexible rubber envelope to distribute pressure around a tyre, while his engineers improved the material bonding the new tread to its casing. The resulting process allowed worn tyres to return to service.\n\nCarver used part of his business wealth to support education and medicine. In 1971, he and his wife Lucille gave Bandag shares to the University of Iowa. The gift supported student assistance, professorships, medical work and cultural facilities.",
    "html": "<p>Roy James Carver turned a German tyre-retreading process into the basis of Bandag, but buying the rights was only the beginning. Making the system commercially reliable required further invention. He devised a flexible rubber envelope to distribute pressure around a tyre, while his engineers improved the material bonding the new tread to its casing. The resulting process allowed worn tyres to return to service.</p>\r\n<p>Carver used part of his business wealth to support education and medicine. In 1971, he and his wife Lucille gave Bandag shares to the University of Iowa. The gift supported student assistance, professorships, medical work and cultural facilities.</p>\r\n",
